WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.750 --> 00:00:10.750
Change lives. Change organizations. Change the world.

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:17.689
Where the GSB is going to have the biggest impact is changing the organizations I’m going to work with. 

00:00:17.689 --> 00:00:20.839
I used to think a lot of leadership as having 

00:00:20.839 --> 00:00:22.820
charisma and strong will 

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.809
and that’s helpful but far from sufficient.  

00:00:26.809 --> 00:00:31.880
It’s really about empathy and understanding the people you’re trying to lead.

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:37.910
Organizations need individuals who take initiative, who are creative, who can problem solve.

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.910
Change organizations

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.110
The world in which our students are going to manage 

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:49.710
is changing at an increasingly rapid rate.  

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:56.000
We equip our students with the ability to understand the global context of management.  

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:02.520
We require that every single student go to a country where they did not grown up and where they have not had significant work experience.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.900
It helps you get out of the classroom and into the real world, 

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:11.210
into a room where somebody who’s an expert in the field and actually be able to understand how you build an oil-well in Brazil, 

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:15.890
how you build a new company in Brazil.  That’s experience that you can’t get in the classroom. 

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:19.980
You don’t only get what you should do 
but also you get ideas on how to do it 

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:25.740
and you learn what kind of real-life impact you can have on people and organizations. 

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:30.180
No matter how much we talk about a country, it’s a completely different thing to actually come to a country.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:36.290
I was a leader on a global study trip to Kenya and Rwanda. 

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:37.590
 We met with President Kegami, the President of Rwanda, 

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:40.230
and Daniel arap Moi, the former President of Kenya.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:42.479
We met the head of marketing of Red Bull.

00:01:42.479 --> 00:01:43.810
A meeting with Bay and Capital. 
We're meeting with Shanda,

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:46.650
the best streaming company in China.

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:49.810
CEOs of Equity Bank Kenya Airways.

00:01:49.820 --> 00:01:52.300
Today we are meeting with the Ministry of Industry and Commerce.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:56.300
And a really modern person in the whole government structure.

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:57.950
He’s also Stanford GSB alum.

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:04.390
Why I think the GSB is an amazing environment, 
it is so people centric in its approach.   

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.390
It was a wonderful experience of two years.  
Number one in some of the experiences that I’ve had in life.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:14.010
Some of the lessons learned from GSB alums were amazing 
with key takeaways that they took  from Stanford 

00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:17.769
and how they’re able to implement them.  

00:02:18.769 --> 00:02:22.980
I mean, change organizations, the way I’m doing it 
is to be a female at this role at this level.  

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.239
To have the confidence to speak my voice.

00:02:26.239 --> 00:02:30.700
Tonight we’ll visit a lot of GS alumni in Shanghai from all industries.  

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:35.019
It seemed nice and they were very 
warm-hearted to give us great career advice.

00:02:35.019 --> 00:02:39.819
We require that on every study trip 
at least one faculty member joins the students.

00:02:40.239 --> 00:02:44.120
When the faculty come on these trips 
we develop that first-hand knowledge 

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:46.830
about these international economies.

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:52.769
See the way local business leaders are responding 
to the particular environment they find themselves in. 

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:58.989
We can bring that experience directly into the 
classroom and say, “Look we’ve spoken with 
these business leaders.”

00:02:58.989 --> 00:03:03.160
The professors offer insights that help 
you quickly get to sort of the root issues of we’re looking at.  

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:05.160
It really helps learning experience. 

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:12.025
Change organizations.

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:18.349
The more you hear from different people 
about how they do things, what they think’s important,  

00:03:18.349 --> 00:03:23.469
you begin to get a sense of what is my leadership style,
what are the things that resonate with me. 

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:25.139
We just get all the opportunities that are out there.

00:03:25.139 --> 00:03:30.099
Guess what, at 28, 29, you can come and be a VP, 
you can work for the CEO, if the next step for you 

00:03:30.099 --> 00:03:34.090
is to be in a senior leadership,  and then change organizations.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:40.499
What I’ve learned here on leadership, on change, 
is going to be hugely valuable to me in the next 3 to 5 years 

00:03:40.499 --> 00:03:43.209
of my career and obviously far beyond that too.  

